<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Montgisard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1177">1177</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Montgisard was fought on 25 November 1177 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. The young King Baldwin IV, with a small force of knights including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a> and the Ibelin brothers, surprised and routed the much larger army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who narrowly escaped back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It was one of the most celebrated crusader victories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-montgisard.jpg" alt="Battle of Montgisard" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles-Philippe Larivière · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_von_Montgisard_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1177 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iv-of-jerusalem">Baldwin IV of Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-count-of-flanders">Philip of Alsace</a>, who had recently arrived on pilgrimage, planned an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> for a naval attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, but nothing came of it. Philip instead joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a>'s expedition against Harim in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> army, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> followed him. This left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> with few troops.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who was planning his own expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, invaded on 18 November. Baldwin, still recovering from malaria, rode out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a> and, according to William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, only 375 knights, and issued an arrière-ban calling up all able-bodied men. Saladin's army looted the kingdom as it advanced, and many of the men it captured were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> on 22 November 1177. Baldwin led his troops out, but seeing how greatly he was outnumbered withdrew into the city. Believing the king no threat, Saladin went on towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and probably let his army spread out to forage. Baldwin ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> to leave Gaza and join him, and he and Raynald broke out of Ascalon along the coast with them. Saladin sent no scouts to watch the city. The two forces met at Mons Gisardi, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>; its exact location is disputed, and Malcolm Barber identifies it with the mound of al-Safiya.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s supply train may have been delayed at a river crossing, and his army was caught unprepared. Baldwin, ill and fighting with bandaged hands, was said to have knelt before the relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> before attacking. Saladin's nephew Taqi ad-Din held effective command on the Egyptian side, and his son Ahmad died in the early fighting. The army was quickly overwhelmed, and Saladin escaped, according to Ralph de Diceto, on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Only a tenth of the sultan's army returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with him; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 8 December. Saladin's army was pursued for twelve miles. The victory was costly for the Christians: Roger de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulins">Moulins</a>, Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, reported 1,100 men killed and 750 wounded. Baldwin founded a Benedictine monastery on the battlefield, dedicated to St. Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, whose feast day fell on the day of the battle.</p><p>The defeat prevented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> from relieving his Syrian vassals during the siege of Harim, which lasted into 1178. Saladin beat Baldwin at the Battle of Marj Ayyun in 1179, but Muslim historians judged Montgisard so severe a defeat that it was redeemed only by the victories of Cresson and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1187.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Montgisard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom won important victories, including King Baldwin IV’s defeat of Saladin at the <b>Battle of Montgisard</b> in 1177, and King Amalric I invaded Egypt several times between 1163 and 1169, briefly forcing it to pay tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ibelin lords Hugh and then Baldwin of Ibelin held it in the later 12th century; Baldwin was among the barons who fought at <b>Montgisard</b> nearby in 1177.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-ramla">Lordship of Ramla</a></li></ul></section>
